The conjecture implies \(\mathrm{P}
e \mathrm{NP}\), but its negation does not automatically imply \(\mathrm{P}
e \mathrm{NP}\). To see the first point, suppose \(\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{NP}\). Then the nontrivial languages {0} and \(0\{0,1\}\)* would both be NP-complete: decide the source problem and output a fixed yes string or no string. Yet a bijection preserving membership cannot map a one-element yes-set onto an infinite yes-set. This elementary obstruction also explains why the unconditional conjecture must be distinguished from its version conditional on \(\mathrm{P}
e \mathrm{NP}\).
